    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d144">

                <pb facs="0331" n="303" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v23p1/pg303"/>

                <head>144. Memorandum of a Telephone Conversation Between the Secretary of State and
                    the Secretary of the Army (Brucker), Washington, May 21, 1957, 12:26 p.m.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d144-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                ><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                                type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1957-05-21T12:26:00-04:00"
                                >May 21, 1957, 12:26 p.m.</date></hi></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">TELEPHONE CALL TO SEC BRUCKER</p>
                <p>The Sec said he was talking with the Pres and he wonders whether we should not
                    say we think we made a mistake and are going to have to reconsider our position
                    and can’t agree so propose it be dealt with through diplomatic channels. Br
                    would not have any power over that. All he can do is with his commander and then
                    he exhausts his power when he tells him to go ahead with the court martial and
                    something else I didn’t get.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d144-f2"/> If a statement were
                    made with ref to the govt level or anything like that B would not have anything
                    to say. The Sec understands that but …<anchor n="3" xml:id="d144-f3"/> Whatever,
                    said B, the Pres does B would of course applaud. The only thing B wants is to
                    get it back on the rails etc. The Sec referred to the cable of 4/26<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d144-f4"/> re no resort to dip <pb facs="0332" n="304"
                        xml:id="pg304"/> channels so he wanted to speak to B. B said we would not
                    have any part in that—B thinks he should not get into anything beyond this step.
                    The Sec said B has to say in light of the facts B believes a mistake was made in
                    the Joint Comm and it is not possible to agree to Japanese jurisdiction and
                    consequently it should be dealt with through govtal channels which the treaty
                    provides. B thought it best not to go into the treaty except to say it is
                    without prejudice. The Sec would like to see what B is sending out and B will
                    have it called over.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d144-f5"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
